/**
 * Day 89 — Phase 5 country pack 5, Southeast Europe: Romania (ROADMAP item 5, third country).
 *
 * Romania had no country timeline. On the globe before the 1815 nations layer nothing showed
 * Dacia, the Roman province, Wallachia, Moldavia or the Principality of Transylvania: the schematic
 * Roman 'mediterranean' ring painted the whole country (and Moldova) from the 1st to the 4th
 * century, and the Ottoman Balkans ring painted Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania as plain
 * Ottoman land from the 1500s to 1800. From 1815 the nations layer draws the Danubian
 * Principalities (id `romania`), so this pack uses other ids and has no keyframe after 1813. The
 * Cucuteni culture, the Getae and the Greek colonies, the migration age, medieval Transylvania
 * and everything after 1813 are timeline items with pins, not outlines.
 *
 * How it is built (docs/DATA.md, "Country-pack globe backfill"):
 *  - 14 new pieces from Natural Earth 1:10m provinces (public domain): Romania's counties grouped
 *    into 12 historical regions (Banat, Crișana, Maramureș–Satu Mare, Sălaj–Cluj, Hunedoara–Alba,
 *    Sibiu, the Burzenland, the Székely land, Bistrița, Suceava, central Moldavia, southern
 *    Moldavia), Moldova west of the Dniester (Bessarabia) and Ukraine's Chernivtsi (northern
 *    Bukovina). Clipped to the globe's own land (shapes 124, 72, 123), simplified as one coverage
 *    and cut back from the Day 87/88 pieces, which this pack reuses unchanged (BG_PIECES: Oltenia,
 *    Muntenia, northern Dobruja, Varna–Dobrich). Generator: et-tools day89-pieces.py (outside the
 *    repo).
 *  - A polity is a dated list of pieces; every change is a one-year step. Pieces on different
 *    modern country lands, and new pieces beside Day 88 pieces, are separate regions.
 *  - Trims (precomputed rings, ROMAN_TRIM_RINGS / OTT_TRIM_RINGS): the Roman 'mediterranean' ring
 *    (70–395) and the Ottoman 'anatolia-balkans' ring (1475–1799) are replaced by held steps — the
 *    entity morphed to each step year, then cut back from the lands north of the Danube (buffered
 *    0.1°, simplified). A held ring does not morph, so its cut edge never sweeps over the pack's
 *    shapes; the other regions of those keyframes are the entity's own shapes morphed at load time.
 *    Rome: Dacia stays free until 105; from 106 to 271 the Roman ring itself draws Roman Dacia (the
 *    Banat, Oltenia and Transylvania) but not the plain, Moldavia or the northern rim, which the old
 *    schematic ring painted; after 271 it stops at the Danube. Ottomans: Wallachia and Moldavia were
 *    tributary principalities, never provinces, and Transylvania was a tributary principality until
 *    the Habsburgs took it (1691–1711); Bukovina (1775) and Bessarabia (1812) became Habsburg and
 *    Russian. So the Ottoman ring stays off all of them, and keeps the Banat and Dobruja.
 *    `applyRomaniaShapeFixes` throws if a source ring changes (checksums).
 *  - Pieces are regions, not surveyed borders (see each polity's confidence reason).
 */

import { ensureClockwise, morphEntityAtYear } from './globe-morph.js';
import { resolveRegionHoles } from './globe-holes.js';
import { mergeRings } from './globe-med-pieces.js';
import { BG_PIECES } from './globe-pack-bulgaria.js';

const W = (title) => ({ title: `Wikipedia — ${title}`, url: `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/${encodeURI(title.replace(/ /g, '_')).replace(/'/g, '%27')}` });

